# Deviation shapes accepted against protocols/gate-run.parley.
#
# `make -C observer check` fails on a deviation shape absent from this
# file, AND on a line here that no longer describes anything in the log.
# The second half is what keeps the check honest: a baseline that only
# grows is a file, not a check.
#
# Every line needs a reason. A shape recorded without one is a modelling
# gap being hidden, which is the opposite of what the observer is for.
#
# Shapes are normalized — run identifiers become `<id>` — so this file
# pins the class of surprise, not the run that produced it, and does not
# rot as gate opens runs.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A resolution recorded AFTER a terminal action (10 runs as of
# 2026-08-23). gate stamped a named human resolution onto a run that had
# already emitted its merge command or its block. Deliberately NOT
# modelled: whether a post-terminal resolution confers merge authority is
# a question the log cannot answer, and adding a branch for it would
# assert an answer. Raised with gate as a finding; run ids are in the
# observer output, not here, so this line survives new occurrences.
events continue past the protocol's end: operator -> gate: resolution

# The same shape with a judge judgment instead of an operator resolution
# (1 run). Same reasoning, same open question, and it is the rarer half —
# worth watching separately in case it is the one that grows.
events continue past the protocol's end: judge -> gate: judgment
